# Rail-Pay

Permissionless FX settlement on Monad. Somebody in London types `send 50k to mum` into a chat,
approves with their face, and a bank account in Lagos receives naira. Local providers compete in a
sealed-bid auction to deliver it, and whatever they do not take comes back to the sender.

Written for: judges, and anyone picking this repo up cold.

Last updated 4 October 2026. 53 commits since 16 September.

---

## 1. The problem, in money

Sending $200 to sub-Saharan Africa costs **8.46%** on average. The global average is **6.36%**.
Those are World Bank figures, Remittance Prices Worldwide Q3 2025, and they have barely moved in a
decade despite three hundred fintechs promising otherwise.

The cost is not in the technology. Moving a dollar between two databases is free. The cost is that
**the price is set by whoever you happened to sign up with**, and you cannot see what anybody else
would have charged. Each provider quotes a rate, hides a margin inside it, and the sender has no way
to make them compete. Signing up takes days, money sits for three, and fees are quoted as "zero" with
the real charge buried in the exchange rate.

Rail does not try to be a cheaper provider. **It makes providers bid against each other for the job,
in the open, on every single transfer.**

---

## 2. How it works

Seven steps. Nothing here is aspirational; all of it runs on Monad testnet today.

1. **A sender escrows dollars.** One EIP-3009 `receiveWithAuthorization` signature moves AUSD into
   `RailCore`. The sender pays no gas: a relayer submits it and is reimbursed from a declared fee.
2. **An auction opens.** Every escrowed transfer is public the moment it exists.
3. **Providers bid sealed.** `commitBid(orderId, commitment)` where the commitment is
   `keccak256(orderId, lp, amount, salt)`. Nobody can see anyone else's price, so nobody can undercut
   by a cent.
4. **They reveal.** `revealBid(orderId, amount, salt)`. The leading bid locks collateral at **110%**
   of itself. A bid whose provider has not staked enough is skipped even if it was lowest.
5. **The winner collects the account number.** Only the winner can, and they prove it with a
   signature over `"Rail payout details\norder: …\nissuedAt: …"`. The relayer checks that address
   against `order.winner` on chain before it hands anything over.
6. **They pay from their own bank.** Entirely off Rail, on their own licence, with a reference like
   `RAILA8BEFD38`. **Rail never touches naira.**
7. **Settlement.** The winner calls `markPaid`, which opens a dispute window. When it closes,
   **anyone** can call `finalize`: the winner receives exactly their bid, and `maxAusd − bid` returns
   to the sender. If they never pay, **anyone** calls `refund` and their collateral is slashed to the
   sender.

### The number that makes it a product

A sender sets a ceiling. Providers undercut each other below it. The difference is returned, every
time, by the contract. The protocol takes no cut of the spread, which is why providers have nothing
to gain by colluding with us: there is no "us" in the price.

---

## 3. The nine invariants

These are in `CLAUDE.md` and every change in this repo is checked against them. They are the reason
the architecture looks the way it does.

1. **Rail never holds, moves, or touches fiat.** No contract, service or bot may custody local
   currency. Providers pay from their own accounts under their own licences. This is the whole
   regulatory argument.
2. **Chat proposes; the sender's own key authorises.** The bot holds no signing key and has no code
   path to any endpoint that moves funds. A test drives every command past a stub relayer and asserts
   `/v1/orders` is never reached.
3. **No PII on-chain.** Bank details appear only as a salted `keccak256` commitment. A ten-digit
   NUBAN without a salt is brute-forced in seconds.
4. **`finalize`, `refund` and `claim` are permissionless.** No owner, pause or allowlist can block
   them. *"What if your backend dies?"* → *"Nothing. Anyone can finalize."*
5. **The sender captures the auction saving.**
6. **A provider always has more locked than it could steal.** 110% collateral, slashed to the wronged
   sender rather than to a treasury.
7. **Reputation is derived, never written.** Counters incremented only by `RailCore`. No setter
   exists and none may be added.
8. **The optimistic layer is never removed.** Attestors can only *accelerate* settlement, never block
   it. If every attestor vanishes, orders still settle.
9. **One signature from the sender.** Order parameters are hashed into the EIP-3009 nonce, so a
   relayer cannot alter the recipient, amount, currency or attestor without invalidating the
   signature it is carrying.

---

## 4. What is built

| Part | Stack | Tests |
|---|---|---|
| `contracts/` | Solidity 0.8.26, Foundry, OpenZeppelin v5 | **47**, 32 adversarial |
| `services/relayer/` | TypeScript, Node type-stripping, viem | **65** |
| `services/matcher/` | TypeScript, provider bidding bot | **12** |
| `bot/` | TypeScript, Telegram + WhatsApp transports | **89** |
| `web/` | Next.js 16, React 19, Tailwind 4, Motion | e2e via Chrome + WebAuthn |
| `cre/` | Chainlink CRE workflow, TypeScript SDK | **15** |
| `indexer/` | Envio HyperIndex | signature check |

**228 automated tests.** No part of the money path is untested.

### Contracts

Three, all under 24KB:

- **`RailCore`** — escrow, commit–reveal auction, settlement, dispute, refund.
- **`LPRegistry`** — provider stake, collateral locking, slashing, append-only reputation.
- **`SignedAttestor`** — layered attestations that can accelerate settlement.

Attack tests are written **before** implementation. Every one carries a one-line comment stating the
attack in plain English: a relayer swapping the attestor, a stranger disputing someone else's
transfer, a forged dispute signature, a winner trying to beat its own dispute window, an attestor
that reverts or burns gas or returns megabytes.

### Services

The relayer quotes, submits, and holds recipient bank details encrypted at rest. It holds MON for
gas and **nothing else**. The matcher is the provider bot: it watches for orders over a WebSocket,
prices them, bids, reveals, collects the account number and confirms payment.

### The chat bot

Nine structured commands: `send`, `add`, `contacts`, `remove`, `balance`, `rate`, `fund`, `about`,
`help`. Deliberately **not** an AI assistant: those are banned on the WhatsApp Business Platform and
are the wrong shape for payments anywhere. Anything unrecognised becomes `help`, never a guess.

Telegram is the pilot transport because it needs no business verification and, with long polling, no
public URL. WhatsApp is the same brain behind a webhook, waiting on Meta. `handle.ts`, `commands.ts`
and `messages/` know nothing about which chat they are speaking into.

---

## 5. Sponsor technology, and what it actually does here

### Monad

Not a deployment target, a **requirement**. A sealed-bid auction needs two rounds, because on a
transparent ledger a bid is public the moment it lands in a block. Two rounds inside a payment is
only affordable where blocks are **302ms** (measured over a 1000-block sample, not quoted) and fees
are near zero. On a chain with twelve-second blocks and dollar gas, a sealed auction on a $37
transfer is absurd. That is why nobody has done this.

### Agora — AUSD

The settlement asset, chosen for **native EIP-3009**. That is what lets a sender who has never
touched crypto sign one authorisation and pay no gas. The EIP-712 domain is read from the asset
itself at signing time rather than hardcoded, because a wrong domain is a signature that verifies
nowhere, discovered at the worst possible moment.

### Mera — passkeys

The entire account layer. `createPasskeyWithPrfOutput` → PRF output → BIP-39 → HDKey
`m/44'/60'/0'/0/{index}` → secp256k1 → a viem account that signs EIP-712. **No private key is ever
stored**; the signing session is ended immediately in every path including failure.

**One passkey, many keys:** index 0 is the spending account and index 1 is a savings account,
derived from the *same* PRF output with no second passkey and no second prompt. An e2e test with a
Chrome virtual authenticator asserts the derivation is deterministic, because if it ever stops being
so, people lose access to their money.

### Chainlink CRE

A decentralised attestor. Today the attestation comes from one signer, which is the weakest part of
the design. The workflow has each DON node query a Nigerian bank aggregator itself and attest only
on **unanimous** agreement.

`identical` consensus is the strictest option available, and it is affordable *because of invariant
8*: a DON that disagrees means the optimistic window settles the order slightly later. The failure
mode is "slower", never "stuck", so the correct bias is to say nothing unless certain. A node that
cannot reach the bank throws rather than voting "not paid" — those are different claims, and
conflating them would slash a provider who did pay.

### Envio

HyperIndex over all four deployments in one dataset. `scripts/check.mjs` verifies all 18 event
signatures against the deployed ABIs, so the indexer cannot silently drift from the contracts.

### Alchemy

The public Monad RPC caps `eth_getLogs` at **100 blocks** — measured, not assumed, after a 413 in a
browser console revealed both the dashboard and the provider feed were silently returning nothing.
A dedicated endpoint removes that cap and the inclusion-latency variance that forced the auction
windows wider than the mechanism needs.

---

## 6. Decisions worth defending

**Why the sender never sees crypto vocabulary.** A grep over the sender UI and every bot string must
return zero hits for `wallet`, `gas`, `crypto`, `token`, `AUSD`, `Monad` and twenty more. Partly
because WhatsApp's commerce policy makes a currency word a ban risk for the number; mostly because
somebody sending money to their mother is not shopping for a financial product.

**Why 150-block windows, when 30 was measured as enough.** Thirty blocks is twelve seconds:
comfortable for a bot, impossible for a person. A window only software can reach makes this a
professionals-only market, which is the thing Rail exists to replace. A bot loses nothing. Settlement
costs ninety seconds instead of twenty-four. That is the right trade for a market anyone can join.

**Why there is nothing to deposit.** A prepaid balance would have to be spendable by something. If
the bot can spend it, invariant 2 is gone and whoever takes over your chat drains it. If you still
sign per order, the deposit saved nothing. So the money stays in the sender's own account until the
authorisation pulls exactly what the transfer costs.

**Why a dispute currently favours the sender.** Without an attestation, a dispute that outlives the
resolution window slashes the provider whether or not they paid. A test asserts this and it passes,
which is the problem: it is why the CRE work is load-bearing rather than decorative. The dispute
window stays short until the attestor is deployed, because lengthening it without one would simply
move who loses.

